1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,063
Welcome to School Computer Science,
Explained. This is Intro to Computer

2
00:00:04,063 --> 00:00:08,465
Science, Unit 2: Data and Binary. We'll
see how computers store numbers, text,

3
00:00:08,465 --> 00:00:13,035
images and sound, all as ones and zeros.
After every lesson, we'll work through a

4
00:00:13,035 --> 00:00:17,493
full example step by step, and we'll
finish by putting the whole unit together.

5
00:00:18,667 --> 00:00:21,227
You count with ten digits, zero through
nine.

6
00:00:21,677 --> 00:00:24,173
A computer only has two. Zero and one.

7
00:00:24,623 --> 00:00:27,759
So how does a computer count past one?
Let's find out.

8
00:00:28,909 --> 00:00:31,725
Inside a computer are billions of tiny
switches.

9
00:00:32,175 --> 00:00:36,292
Each switch is either off or on. We write
off as zero, and on as one.

10
00:00:36,742 --> 00:00:40,796
A single zero or one is called a bit,
short for binary digit.

11
00:00:41,946 --> 00:00:44,420
First, let's look at how our normal
numbers work.

12
00:00:44,870 --> 00:00:47,110
Take the number three hundred forty five.

13
00:00:47,560 --> 00:00:52,638
The five means five ones. The four means
four tens. The three means three hundreds.

14
00:00:53,088 --> 00:00:57,994
Each place is worth ten times the place to
its right. That's why we call it base ten.

15
00:00:59,144 --> 00:01:01,683
Binary works the same way, but with base
two.

16
00:01:02,133 --> 00:01:05,077
Each place is worth two times the place to
its right.

17
00:01:05,527 --> 00:01:10,929
So instead of ones, tens and hundreds, the
places are one, two, four, eight, sixteen,

18
00:01:10,929 --> 00:01:11,564
and so on.

19
00:01:12,714 --> 00:01:13,696
Now let's count.

20
00:01:14,146 --> 00:01:19,010
Zero. One. Now we're out of digits, so we
carry, just like going from nine to ten.

21
00:01:19,460 --> 00:01:24,132
One zero is two. One one is three. Carry
again: one zero zero is four.

22
00:01:24,582 --> 00:01:29,126
Five, six, seven. And carrying once more,
one zero zero zero is eight.

23
00:01:30,276 --> 00:01:33,710
To read a binary number, add up the places
that hold a one.

24
00:01:34,160 --> 00:01:35,696
Take one zero one one.

25
00:01:36,146 --> 00:01:39,581
There's a one in the eights place, the
twos place, and the ones place.

26
00:01:40,031 --> 00:01:45,492
Eight plus two plus one is eleven. So one
zero one one in binary is eleven.

27
00:01:46,642 --> 00:01:49,586
Going the other way, start with the
biggest place that fits.

28
00:01:50,036 --> 00:01:52,980
Let's convert thirteen. Eight fits,
leaving five.

29
00:01:53,430 --> 00:01:58,444
Four fits, leaving one. Two doesn't fit,
so that place gets a zero. And one fits.

30
00:01:58,894 --> 00:02:01,134
So thirteen is one one zero one.

31
00:02:02,284 --> 00:02:06,252
Computers usually group bits in eights.
Eight bits make one byte.

32
00:02:06,702 --> 00:02:11,310
With eight bits, the biggest number is all
ones, which is two hundred fifty five.

33
00:02:11,760 --> 00:02:15,237
Counting zero, that's two hundred fifty
six different values.

34
00:02:16,387 --> 00:02:20,184
Now you try. What is one zero zero one
zero in decimal?

35
00:02:20,634 --> 00:02:21,829
Pause the video now.

36
00:02:22,279 --> 00:02:24,647
The answer is eighteen: sixteen plus two.

37
00:02:25,097 --> 00:02:28,084
Next, let's work through a full example,
step by step.

38
00:02:29,233 --> 00:02:33,478
Let's convert a number to binary, step by
step, and then check our answer.

39
00:02:34,628 --> 00:02:38,170
The problem: write forty five in binary,
using six bits.

40
00:02:38,620 --> 00:02:40,817
Then convert it back to make sure it's
right.

41
00:02:41,967 --> 00:02:46,559
Here's the method. List the place values:
thirty two, sixteen, eight, four, two,

42
00:02:46,559 --> 00:02:46,788
one.

43
00:02:47,238 --> 00:02:51,718
Start at the biggest place. If it fits in
what's left, write a one and subtract it.

44
00:02:52,168 --> 00:02:55,475
If it doesn't fit, write a zero. Then move
to the next place.

45
00:02:56,625 --> 00:03:00,380
Thirty two fits in forty five. Write one,
and thirteen is left.

46
00:03:00,830 --> 00:03:03,454
Sixteen doesn't fit in thirteen. Write
zero.

47
00:03:03,904 --> 00:03:05,760
Eight fits. Write one, five left.

48
00:03:06,210 --> 00:03:07,874
Four fits. Write one, one left.

49
00:03:08,324 --> 00:03:09,860
Two doesn't fit. Zero.

50
00:03:10,310 --> 00:03:12,464
One fits. Write one, and nothing is left.

51
00:03:13,614 --> 00:03:16,857
So forty five is one zero one one zero
one.

52
00:03:17,307 --> 00:03:21,638
Check it: thirty two plus eight plus four
plus one is forty five. It works.

53
00:03:22,088 --> 00:03:26,842
Common mistake: skipping the zeros. Every
place gets a bit, even when it doesn't

54
00:03:26,842 --> 00:03:27,080
fit.

55
00:03:28,230 --> 00:03:31,686
Your turn. Write twenty six in binary,
using five bits.

56
00:03:32,136 --> 00:03:33,330
Pause the video now.

57
00:03:33,780 --> 00:03:38,452
One one zero one zero. Sixteen plus eight
plus two is twenty six.

58
00:03:38,902 --> 00:03:41,953
Next, hexadecimal, a shorter way to write
binary.

59
00:03:43,103 --> 00:03:47,498
Here's a byte in binary: one one zero one,
zero one one zero.

60
00:03:47,948 --> 00:03:50,337
Eight digits, and it's easy to lose your
place.

61
00:03:50,787 --> 00:03:55,438
Programmers use a shorter way to write the
same thing. It's called hexadecimal.

62
00:03:56,588 --> 00:04:00,193
Hexadecimal is base sixteen, so it needs
sixteen digits.

63
00:04:00,643 --> 00:04:02,627
Zero through nine give us ten of them.

64
00:04:03,077 --> 00:04:08,319
For the other six, we borrow letters. A is
ten, B is eleven, C is twelve, D is

65
00:04:08,319 --> 00:04:11,141
thirteen, E is fourteen, and F is fifteen.

66
00:04:12,291 --> 00:04:13,891
Here's why hex is so useful.

67
00:04:14,341 --> 00:04:19,721
Four bits can make exactly sixteen
patterns, from zero zero zero zero up to

68
00:04:19,721 --> 00:04:20,869
one one one one.

69
00:04:21,319 --> 00:04:26,780
That's the same as one hex digit. So every
group of four bits becomes one hex digit.

70
00:04:27,930 --> 00:04:31,216
Let's convert our byte. Split it into two
groups of four.

71
00:04:31,666 --> 00:04:37,063
One one zero one is eight plus four plus
one, which is thirteen. In hex, that's D.

72
00:04:37,513 --> 00:04:40,713
Zero one one zero is four plus two, which
is six.

73
00:04:41,163 --> 00:04:44,896
So the byte is D six in hex. Two
characters instead of eight.

74
00:04:46,046 --> 00:04:50,840
Hex has place values too, but each place
is worth sixteen times the one to its

75
00:04:50,840 --> 00:04:51,209
right.

76
00:04:51,659 --> 00:04:56,246
In D six, the D is in the sixteens place,
and the six is in the ones place.

77
00:04:56,696 --> 00:05:01,509
Thirteen times sixteen is two hundred
eight. Add six, and you get two hundred

78
00:05:01,509 --> 00:05:02,072
fourteen.

79
00:05:03,222 --> 00:05:05,120
You've probably seen hex already.

80
00:05:05,570 --> 00:05:11,887
Web colors are written in hex. Pound F F
eight eight zero zero means red F F, green

81
00:05:11,887 --> 00:05:14,018
eight eight, blue zero zero.

82
00:05:14,468 --> 00:05:19,674
F F is two hundred fifty five, the most
red possible. So this color is orange.

83
00:05:20,124 --> 00:05:25,624
Hex also shows up in memory addresses and
error codes, anywhere people need to read

84
00:05:25,624 --> 00:05:26,353
raw binary.

85
00:05:27,503 --> 00:05:32,196
Your turn. Convert one zero one zero, zero
zero one one to hex.

86
00:05:32,646 --> 00:05:33,841
Pause the video now.

87
00:05:34,291 --> 00:05:39,240
One zero one zero is ten, which is A. Zero
zero one one is three.

88
00:05:39,690 --> 00:05:42,677
Next, let's work through a full example,
step by step.

89
00:05:43,827 --> 00:05:47,838
Let's decode a real web color from hex
into red, green and blue.

90
00:05:48,988 --> 00:05:53,724
The problem: a web page uses the color
pound three C, nine F, D two.

91
00:05:54,174 --> 00:05:58,227
How much red, green and blue is in it,
from zero to two fifty five?

92
00:05:59,377 --> 00:06:03,964
Here's the method. Split the six digits
into three pairs: red, green, blue.

93
00:06:04,414 --> 00:06:08,744
For each pair, multiply the first digit by
sixteen, and add the second.

94
00:06:09,194 --> 00:06:11,712
Remember, A is ten, up to F, which is
fifteen.

95
00:06:12,862 --> 00:06:17,427
Red is three C. Three times sixteen is
forty eight, plus twelve is sixty.

96
00:06:17,877 --> 00:06:23,640
Green is nine F. Nine times sixteen is one
forty four, plus fifteen is one fifty

97
00:06:23,640 --> 00:06:24,000
nine.

98
00:06:24,450 --> 00:06:29,649
Blue is D two. D is thirteen. Thirteen
times sixteen is two hundred eight, plus

99
00:06:29,649 --> 00:06:30,636
two is two ten.

100
00:06:31,786 --> 00:06:36,800
So the color is red sixty, green one fifty
nine, blue two ten: a sky blue.

101
00:06:37,250 --> 00:06:42,856
Common mistake: adding the digits instead
of multiplying the first by sixteen. Three

102
00:06:42,856 --> 00:06:43,991
C is not fifteen.

103
00:06:45,141 --> 00:06:50,538
Your turn. What is the red value in the
color pound eight zero, zero zero, zero

104
00:06:50,538 --> 00:06:50,880
zero?

105
00:06:51,330 --> 00:06:52,524
Pause the video now.

106
00:06:52,974 --> 00:06:57,604
Eight times sixteen plus zero, which is
one twenty eight. It's a dark red.

107
00:06:58,054 --> 00:06:59,846
Next, how computers store text.

108
00:07:00,996 --> 00:07:04,324
A computer can only store numbers. Ones
and zeros.

109
00:07:04,774 --> 00:07:07,803
So when you type the letter A, what
actually gets saved?

110
00:07:08,253 --> 00:07:10,365
Let's find out how text becomes numbers.

111
00:07:11,515 --> 00:07:15,099
The trick is an agreement. Every character
gets its own number.

112
00:07:15,549 --> 00:07:20,899
Capital A is sixty five. B is sixty six, C
is sixty seven, and so on through the

113
00:07:20,899 --> 00:07:21,501
alphabet.

114
00:07:21,951 --> 00:07:26,794
Lowercase letters start at ninety seven.
Even a space has a number: thirty two.

115
00:07:27,944 --> 00:07:30,931
Let's encode the word Hi, with an
exclamation mark.

116
00:07:31,381 --> 00:07:36,625
H is seventy two. Lowercase i is one
hundred five. The exclamation mark is

117
00:07:36,625 --> 00:07:37,546
thirty three.

118
00:07:37,996 --> 00:07:40,748
In memory, those are just three bytes of
binary.

119
00:07:41,898 --> 00:07:45,951
This table is called ASCII. It was created
in the nineteen sixties.

120
00:07:46,401 --> 00:07:51,346
ASCII uses seven bits, so it has room for
one hundred twenty eight characters:

121
00:07:51,346 --> 00:07:55,276
English letters, digits, punctuation, and
a few control codes.

122
00:07:55,726 --> 00:08:00,966
Here's a surprise. The character seven is
stored as fifty five, not as the number

123
00:08:00,966 --> 00:08:03,683
seven. A digit you type is text, not math.

124
00:08:04,833 --> 00:08:08,097
But one hundred twenty eight characters
isn't enough for the world.

125
00:08:08,547 --> 00:08:13,198
What about accented letters, Greek,
Russian, Japanese, Chinese, or emoji?

126
00:08:13,648 --> 00:08:18,449
Unicode solves this. It gives a number,
called a code point, to over one hundred

127
00:08:18,449 --> 00:08:21,989
fifty thousand characters from nearly
every writing system.

128
00:08:22,439 --> 00:08:26,258
A is still sixty five, so ASCII fits right
inside Unicode.

129
00:08:27,408 --> 00:08:30,757
To store Unicode, most of the web uses UTF
eight.

130
00:08:31,207 --> 00:08:34,322
In UTF eight, a character takes one to
four bytes.

131
00:08:34,772 --> 00:08:40,263
Plain English letters still use one byte,
exactly like ASCII. Other characters use

132
00:08:40,263 --> 00:08:42,004
more. An emoji takes four.

133
00:08:43,154 --> 00:08:47,485
Your turn. Decode these numbers: sixty
seven, sixty five, eighty four.

134
00:08:47,935 --> 00:08:49,129
Pause the video now.

135
00:08:49,579 --> 00:08:54,529
Sixty seven is C, sixty five is A, and
eighty four is T. The word is cat.

136
00:08:54,979 --> 00:08:57,965
Next, let's work through a full example,
step by step.

137
00:08:59,115 --> 00:09:02,315
Let's turn a word into the exact bits a
computer stores.

138
00:09:03,465 --> 00:09:07,220
The problem: encode the word Go, with a
capital G, in ASCII.

139
00:09:07,670 --> 00:09:11,062
Give each letter's number, its binary, and
the total size.

140
00:09:12,212 --> 00:09:15,604
Here's the method. Find each letter's
position in the alphabet.

141
00:09:16,054 --> 00:09:19,894
Capital letters start at sixty five,
lowercase at ninety seven.

142
00:09:20,344 --> 00:09:22,114
Then write each number as eight bits.

143
00:09:23,264 --> 00:09:27,360
G is the seventh letter. Sixty five plus
six is seventy one.

144
00:09:27,810 --> 00:09:34,407
Seventy one is sixty four plus four plus
two plus one: zero one zero zero, zero one

145
00:09:34,407 --> 00:09:35,042
one one.

146
00:09:35,492 --> 00:09:40,356
o is the fifteenth letter. Ninety seven
plus fourteen is one hundred eleven.

147
00:09:40,806 --> 00:09:46,654
One eleven is sixty four plus thirty two
plus eight plus four plus two plus one:

148
00:09:46,654 --> 00:09:49,212
zero one one zero, one one one one.

149
00:09:50,362 --> 00:09:54,010
The word Go is stored as two bytes,
sixteen bits in total.

150
00:09:54,460 --> 00:09:59,564
Common mistake: using sixty five for
lowercase letters. Lowercase starts at

151
00:09:59,564 --> 00:10:02,694
ninety seven, so G and g have different
codes.

152
00:10:03,844 --> 00:10:06,639
Your turn. What is the ASCII number of a
capital E?

153
00:10:07,089 --> 00:10:08,284
Pause the video now.

154
00:10:08,734 --> 00:10:12,531
Sixty nine. E is the fifth letter, so
sixty five plus four.

155
00:10:12,981 --> 00:10:14,816
Next, how images become numbers.

156
00:10:15,966 --> 00:10:18,675
Every photo on your phone is stored as
numbers.

157
00:10:19,125 --> 00:10:23,520
But a picture isn't text, and it isn't a
single number. So how does that work?

158
00:10:23,970 --> 00:10:24,909
Let's zoom in.

159
00:10:26,059 --> 00:10:30,773
Zoom in far enough on any digital image,
and you'll see a grid of tiny squares.

160
00:10:31,223 --> 00:10:34,445
Each square is called a pixel, short for
picture element.

161
00:10:34,895 --> 00:10:40,164
A pixel shows exactly one color. The whole
image is just a grid of colored pixels.

162
00:10:41,314 --> 00:10:43,639
Let's start simple, with only black and
white.

163
00:10:44,089 --> 00:10:47,865
One bit per pixel is enough. Zero for
black, one for white.

164
00:10:48,315 --> 00:10:53,377
So this little eight by eight smiley face
is just sixty four bits, written row by

165
00:10:53,377 --> 00:10:53,627
row.

166
00:10:54,777 --> 00:10:58,084
For color, screens mix three lights: red,
green, and blue.

167
00:10:58,534 --> 00:11:03,437
Each one gets a brightness from zero to
two hundred fifty five, which fits in one

168
00:11:03,437 --> 00:11:03,739
byte.

169
00:11:04,189 --> 00:11:09,261
Full red with no green or blue is pure
red. Red and green together make yellow.

170
00:11:09,261 --> 00:11:11,123
All three at full make white.

171
00:11:12,273 --> 00:11:16,262
Three bytes per pixel gives over sixteen
million possible colors.

172
00:11:16,712 --> 00:11:21,736
That's two hundred fifty six, times two
hundred fifty six, times two hundred fifty

173
00:11:21,736 --> 00:11:21,981
six.

174
00:11:23,131 --> 00:11:24,475
Now let's count the cost.

175
00:11:24,925 --> 00:11:29,853
A typical phone photo is about four
thousand by three thousand pixels. That's

176
00:11:29,853 --> 00:11:31,261
twelve million pixels.

177
00:11:31,711 --> 00:11:36,550
At three bytes each, that's thirty six
million bytes, about thirty six megabytes,

178
00:11:36,550 --> 00:11:37,386
for one photo.

179
00:11:37,836 --> 00:11:41,697
That's why images are usually compressed,
which is coming up soon.

180
00:11:42,847 --> 00:11:47,327
Resolution is the number of pixels. More
pixels means finer detail.

181
00:11:47,777 --> 00:11:52,416
Color depth is the number of bits per
pixel. More bits means more possible

182
00:11:52,416 --> 00:11:52,855
colors.

183
00:11:53,305 --> 00:11:56,718
Both make images look better, and both
make files bigger.

184
00:11:57,868 --> 00:12:03,403
Your turn. A tiny image is ten pixels wide
and ten pixels tall, with three bytes per

185
00:12:03,403 --> 00:12:03,799
pixel.

186
00:12:04,249 --> 00:12:06,681
How many bytes is it? Pause the video now.

187
00:12:07,131 --> 00:12:12,229
Ten times ten is one hundred pixels. Times
three bytes is three hundred bytes.

188
00:12:12,679 --> 00:12:15,666
Next, let's work through a full example,
step by step.

189
00:12:16,816 --> 00:12:21,424
Let's work out exactly how much space a
photo takes, before any compression.

190
00:12:22,574 --> 00:12:27,737
The problem: a webcam image is six forty
by four eighty pixels, in full color.

191
00:12:28,187 --> 00:12:30,597
How many bytes, and about how many
megabytes?

192
00:12:31,747 --> 00:12:35,737
Here's the method. Multiply width by
height to get the number of pixels.

193
00:12:36,187 --> 00:12:39,749
Full color is twenty four bits, which is
three bytes per pixel.

194
00:12:40,199 --> 00:12:43,591
Multiply, then divide by about a million
for megabytes.

195
00:12:44,741 --> 00:12:49,648
Six forty times four eighty is three
hundred seven thousand two hundred pixels.

196
00:12:50,098 --> 00:12:54,404
Each pixel takes three bytes, so that's
nine hundred twenty one thousand six

197
00:12:54,404 --> 00:12:55,197
hundred bytes.

198
00:12:55,647 --> 00:12:59,316
Divide by a million: about zero point nine
two megabytes.

199
00:13:00,466 --> 00:13:05,734
One webcam frame is almost a megabyte. A
video at thirty frames a second would be

200
00:13:05,734 --> 00:13:08,530
nearly twenty eight megabytes every
second!

201
00:13:08,980 --> 00:13:12,479
That's why images and video are almost
always compressed.

202
00:13:13,629 --> 00:13:18,723
Your turn. A black and white icon is
thirty two by thirty two pixels, with one

203
00:13:18,723 --> 00:13:21,074
bit per pixel. How many bytes is it?

204
00:13:21,524 --> 00:13:22,719
Pause the video now.

205
00:13:23,169 --> 00:13:27,490
Thirty two times thirty two is one
thousand twenty four bits. Divide by

206
00:13:27,490 --> 00:13:29,803
eight: one hundred twenty eight bytes.

207
00:13:30,253 --> 00:13:32,003
Next, how sound becomes numbers.

208
00:13:33,153 --> 00:13:37,612
Music, voices, this narration. A computer
stores all of it as numbers.

209
00:13:38,062 --> 00:13:42,499
But sound is a smooth wave in the air. How
do you turn a wave into numbers?

210
00:13:42,949 --> 00:13:44,229
The answer is sampling.

211
00:13:45,379 --> 00:13:51,194
Sound is a vibration. A microphone turns
it into a signal that rises and falls over

212
00:13:51,194 --> 00:13:51,544
time.

213
00:13:51,994 --> 00:13:56,730
This curve is smooth and continuous. It
has a value at every single instant.

214
00:13:57,180 --> 00:14:02,133
A computer can't store infinitely many
values, so it measures the wave at regular

215
00:14:02,133 --> 00:14:03,111
moments instead.

216
00:14:04,261 --> 00:14:08,595
Each measurement is called a sample. It
records the height of the wave at that

217
00:14:08,595 --> 00:14:09,040
instant.

218
00:14:09,490 --> 00:14:13,436
Store the samples in order, and you have
the sound as a list of numbers.

219
00:14:13,886 --> 00:14:17,513
To play it back, the computer rebuilds the
wave from those samples.

220
00:14:18,663 --> 00:14:21,202
How often you sample is called the sample
rate.

221
00:14:21,652 --> 00:14:26,644
Sample too slowly, and you miss the fast
wiggles. The wave you rebuild looks wrong.

222
00:14:27,094 --> 00:14:30,507
Sample faster, and the copy gets closer to
the original.

223
00:14:31,657 --> 00:14:36,670
Music on a CD uses forty four thousand one
hundred samples every second.

224
00:14:37,120 --> 00:14:40,640
That's fast enough to capture every pitch
most humans can hear.

225
00:14:41,790 --> 00:14:45,353
The second setting is bit depth: how many
bits store each sample.

226
00:14:45,803 --> 00:14:50,427
With only a few bits, each sample has to
snap to a few allowed levels, and the

227
00:14:50,427 --> 00:14:51,435
sound gets rough.

228
00:14:51,885 --> 00:14:57,380
CD audio uses sixteen bits, which allows
sixty five thousand five hundred thirty

229
00:14:57,380 --> 00:14:58,136
six levels.

230
00:14:59,286 --> 00:15:01,846
So how big is one minute of CD audio?

231
00:15:02,296 --> 00:15:07,416
Forty four thousand one hundred samples,
times two bytes, times two channels for

232
00:15:07,416 --> 00:15:09,208
stereo, times sixty seconds.

233
00:15:09,658 --> 00:15:13,754
That's about ten and a half million bytes,
roughly ten megabytes per minute.

234
00:15:14,904 --> 00:15:20,002
Your turn. A recording uses ten thousand
samples per second, with one byte per

235
00:15:20,002 --> 00:15:21,048
sample, in mono.

236
00:15:21,498 --> 00:15:24,570
How many bytes for ten seconds? Pause the
video now.

237
00:15:25,020 --> 00:15:28,966
Ten thousand times one times ten is one
hundred thousand bytes.

238
00:15:29,416 --> 00:15:32,403
Next, let's work through a full example,
step by step.

239
00:15:33,553 --> 00:15:37,863
Let's calculate the size of a voice
message, and see how the settings change

240
00:15:37,863 --> 00:15:38,033
it.

241
00:15:39,183 --> 00:15:42,020
The problem: a voice message lasts thirty
seconds.

242
00:15:42,470 --> 00:15:48,011
It's recorded at sixteen thousand samples
per second, two bytes per sample, in mono.

243
00:15:48,011 --> 00:15:48,934
How big is it?

244
00:15:50,084 --> 00:15:55,433
Here's the formula. Size equals samples
per second, times bytes per sample, times

245
00:15:55,433 --> 00:15:57,018
channels, times seconds.

246
00:15:58,168 --> 00:16:02,413
Sixteen thousand times two is thirty two
thousand bytes each second.

247
00:16:02,863 --> 00:16:06,106
Mono means one channel, so still thirty
two thousand.

248
00:16:06,556 --> 00:16:11,228
Times thirty seconds: nine hundred sixty
thousand bytes, just under a megabyte.

249
00:16:11,678 --> 00:16:16,812
If it were CD quality stereo, forty four
thousand one hundred samples, two bytes,

250
00:16:16,812 --> 00:16:20,552
two channels, it would be about five point
three megabytes.

251
00:16:21,702 --> 00:16:27,505
The voice message is about zero point nine
six megabytes. The same thirty seconds at

252
00:16:27,505 --> 00:16:30,406
CD quality is more than five times bigger.

253
00:16:30,856 --> 00:16:34,611
Voice doesn't need CD quality, so apps
choose smaller settings.

254
00:16:35,761 --> 00:16:39,217
Your turn. The same message, but in
stereo. How many bytes?

255
00:16:39,667 --> 00:16:40,862
Pause the video now.

256
00:16:41,312 --> 00:16:44,320
Double: one million nine hundred twenty
thousand bytes.

257
00:16:44,770 --> 00:16:46,903
Next, how compression makes files smaller.

258
00:16:48,053 --> 00:16:52,938
A single phone photo can take thirty six
megabytes. A minute of music, ten.

259
00:16:53,388 --> 00:16:56,738
Yet the files on your phone are usually
much smaller than that.

260
00:16:57,188 --> 00:17:01,284
The trick is compression: storing the same
information in fewer bits.

261
00:17:02,434 --> 00:17:05,015
Compression works because data often
repeats.

262
00:17:05,465 --> 00:17:09,604
Look at this row of pixels: four white,
three black, two white, one black.

263
00:17:10,054 --> 00:17:14,640
Instead of storing every pixel, we can
store how many in a row, and what color.

264
00:17:15,090 --> 00:17:20,274
Four W, three B, two W, one B. That's
called run length encoding.

265
00:17:21,424 --> 00:17:25,094
The original took ten symbols. The
compressed version takes eight.

266
00:17:25,544 --> 00:17:30,258
With long runs, like a clear blue sky in a
photo, the savings get much bigger.

267
00:17:30,708 --> 00:17:34,963
And nothing is lost. We can rebuild the
exact original from the compressed

268
00:17:34,963 --> 00:17:35,423
version.

269
00:17:36,573 --> 00:17:39,325
Compression where nothing is lost is
called lossless.

270
00:17:39,775 --> 00:17:42,591
Zip files and P N G images are lossless.

271
00:17:43,041 --> 00:17:47,930
Text, code, and spreadsheets must use
lossless compression. Change a single bit,

272
00:17:47,930 --> 00:17:50,252
and a number or a word could be wrong.

273
00:17:51,402 --> 00:17:56,506
The other kind is lossy compression. It
throws away detail you probably won't

274
00:17:56,506 --> 00:17:56,970
notice.

275
00:17:57,420 --> 00:18:03,214
A J P E G photo blends tiny color
differences together. An M P three removes

276
00:18:03,214 --> 00:18:05,654
sounds your ear can barely hear.

277
00:18:06,104 --> 00:18:10,819
Lossy files can be ten times smaller, but
the lost detail is gone for good.

278
00:18:11,969 --> 00:18:13,164
So which should you use?

279
00:18:13,614 --> 00:18:16,430
If you need the exact original back,
choose lossless.

280
00:18:16,880 --> 00:18:22,146
If you need the smallest file, and small
changes are fine, choose lossy. That's why

281
00:18:22,146 --> 00:18:24,240
streaming video and music use it.

282
00:18:25,390 --> 00:18:31,363
Your turn. Compress this row with run
length encoding: six W, then three B, then

283
00:18:31,363 --> 00:18:31,811
one W.

284
00:18:32,261 --> 00:18:33,456
Pause the video now.

285
00:18:33,906 --> 00:18:36,978
The answer is six W, three B, one W.

286
00:18:37,428 --> 00:18:40,414
Next, let's work through a full example,
step by step.

287
00:18:41,564 --> 00:18:46,215
Let's compress a small image with run
length encoding, and measure how much we

288
00:18:46,215 --> 00:18:46,513
save.

289
00:18:47,663 --> 00:18:53,637
The problem: an icon has three rows, each
ten pixels. W is white, and B is black.

290
00:18:54,087 --> 00:18:57,991
Compress each row with run length
encoding, and compare the sizes.

291
00:18:59,141 --> 00:19:01,722
Here's the method. Read the row from left
to right.

292
00:19:02,172 --> 00:19:04,839
Count how many of the same color appear in
a row.

293
00:19:05,289 --> 00:19:08,041
Write the count, then the color. Then
start a new run.

294
00:19:09,191 --> 00:19:13,052
Row one is ten whites: ten W. Two symbols
instead of ten.

295
00:19:13,502 --> 00:19:18,323
Row two: three W, four B, three W. Six
symbols instead of ten.

296
00:19:18,773 --> 00:19:24,102
Row three switches color every pixel: ten
runs of one. That's twenty symbols, worse

297
00:19:24,102 --> 00:19:25,515
than the original ten!

298
00:19:26,665 --> 00:19:31,912
In total: twenty eight symbols compressed,
versus thirty original. Rows one and two

299
00:19:31,912 --> 00:19:33,619
shrank, but row three grew.

300
00:19:34,069 --> 00:19:39,544
Run length encoding only helps when the
data has long runs. And it's lossless: we

301
00:19:39,544 --> 00:19:41,707
can rebuild every pixel exactly.

302
00:19:42,857 --> 00:19:46,974
Your turn. Encode this row: B B B B B W W
B B B.

303
00:19:47,424 --> 00:19:48,619
Pause the video now.

304
00:19:49,069 --> 00:19:51,031
Five B, two W, three B.

305
00:19:51,481 --> 00:19:53,465
Next, let's put the whole unit together.

306
00:19:54,615 --> 00:19:58,412
You've learned how computers store
numbers, text, images and sound.

307
00:19:58,862 --> 00:20:03,826
Now let's use all of it on one real
message: a photo, a caption, and a voice

308
00:20:03,826 --> 00:20:04,153
note.

309
00:20:05,303 --> 00:20:10,338
Here's the plan. The caption uses ASCII
and binary. Hex shows the bytes compactly.

310
00:20:10,788 --> 00:20:15,334
The photo is pixels, the voice note is
samples, and compression makes it all

311
00:20:15,334 --> 00:20:16,590
small enough to send.

312
00:20:17,740 --> 00:20:23,106
Step one: the caption. H is seventy two,
lowercase i is one hundred five, and the

313
00:20:23,106 --> 00:20:25,292
exclamation mark is thirty three.

314
00:20:25,742 --> 00:20:29,561
Each becomes one byte of binary. Three
characters, three bytes.

315
00:20:30,711 --> 00:20:36,012
Step two: programmers read bytes in hex.
Split each byte into two groups of four

316
00:20:36,012 --> 00:20:36,343
bits.

317
00:20:36,793 --> 00:20:39,438
So the caption is four eight, six nine,
two one.

318
00:20:40,588 --> 00:20:45,867
Step three: the photo is one thousand by
one thousand pixels, each with three bytes

319
00:20:45,867 --> 00:20:47,330
of red, green and blue.

320
00:20:47,780 --> 00:20:50,233
That's three million bytes, about three
megabytes.

321
00:20:51,383 --> 00:20:56,393
Step four: a ten second voice note at
sixteen thousand samples per second, two

322
00:20:56,393 --> 00:20:57,484
bytes each, mono.

323
00:20:57,934 --> 00:21:00,025
That's three hundred twenty thousand
bytes.

324
00:21:01,175 --> 00:21:05,804
Step five: compression. Text must be
lossless, so the caption stays exact.

325
00:21:06,254 --> 00:21:10,840
The photo and the voice can use lossy
compression, about ten times smaller, and

326
00:21:10,840 --> 00:21:12,697
you won't notice the difference.

327
00:21:13,147 --> 00:21:17,793
The whole message drops from about three
point three megabytes to about a third of

328
00:21:17,793 --> 00:21:18,416
a megabyte.

329
00:21:19,566 --> 00:21:22,532
Your turn. Why can't the caption use lossy
compression?

330
00:21:22,982 --> 00:21:24,176
Pause the video now.

331
00:21:24,626 --> 00:21:29,654
Because changing even one bit would change
a letter. Text has to come back exactly,

332
00:21:29,654 --> 00:21:31,048
so it must be lossless.

333
00:21:31,498 --> 00:21:34,996
That's the end of Unit 2. Next, Unit 3:
algorithms.

334
00:21:36,146 --> 00:21:40,575
If this helped, subscribe to School STEM,
Explained, so you don't miss the next

335
00:21:40,575 --> 00:21:40,968
lesson.

336
00:21:41,418 --> 00:21:42,250
See you there.

